The reason the app was still reading as "Streamlit" and showing the Streamlit logo on your mobile device comes down to how Streamlit serves web applications:

The Emoji Limitation in st.set_page_config: When page_icon="🧴" (an emoji) was passed to Streamlit, Streamlit generated a generic browser favicon. iOS Safari and Android Chrome ignore emojis when saving a Web App to the home screen—instead, they take a screenshot or fall back to the host platform's default icon (Streamlit's red/white crown logo).

Body vs. Head Placement: Streamlit's st.markdown('<head>...</head>', unsafe_allow_html=True) puts those HTML tags inside an inner <div> inside <body>. iOS Safari and Android Chrome only inspect the actual root <head> of the browser window when generating the home-screen bookmark.

Aggressive Mobile Browser Caching: If you visited the URL or bookmarked it previously, Safari and Chrome cache the initial title and icon until the cache is cleared or a new private tab is opened.

