How do I roll back changes in Github?

Rolling back changes in GitHub depends on whether you want to undo changes locally before pushing, revert a commit that has already been pushed, or reset your repository to an earlier state.

Here are the most common ways to do it, depending on your situation:

Method 1: Revert a Pushed Commit (Safest for Public/Collaborative Repos)
If you already pushed your code to GitHub and want to undo a specific commit without erasing history, use git revert. This creates a new commit that undoes the changes of the bad commit.

Find the commit hash you want to undo (you can view this in your GitHub repository's commit history or by running git log).

Run the revert command in your terminal:

Bash
git revert <commit-hash>
Push the change to GitHub:

Bash
git push origin main
Method 2: Undo Uncommitted Local Changes (Discard Local Edits)
If you made changes on your computer but haven't committed them yet, and you want to completely throw them away and return to the last saved state:

Discard changes to a specific file:

Bash
git checkout -- <filename>
# or in newer Git versions:
git restore <filename>
Discard ALL uncommitted local changes (Careful: this deletes unsaved work):

Bash
git reset --hard HEAD
Method 3: Reset Local History to a Previous Commit
If you made several local commits that you want to wipe out entirely, moving your branch back in time to an older commit:

Look up your commit history to find the hash of the good commit you want to return to:

Bash
git log --oneline
Reset your branch to that commit:

Soft Reset (Keeps your changed files in your working directory so you can edit them):

Bash
git reset --soft <commit-hash>
Hard Reset (Wipes out all commits and changes made after that point—warning: destructive):

Bash
git reset --hard <commit-hash>
If you already pushed the bad commits to GitHub and are force-resetting your main branch, you will need to force push:

Bash
git push origin main --force
Method 4: Roll Back Directly on the GitHub Website
If you don't want to use the command line and want to undo a pull request or file change directly in your browser:

Go to your repository on GitHub.com.

Click on Pull Requests, and open the merged PR you want to undo.

Scroll down to the bottom where it shows the merge event. If it was recently merged, you will see a Revert button. Click it, and GitHub will automatically create a pull request to roll back those changes.

