Why It Still Shows "Streamlit" and the Streamlit Logo
When hosting on Streamlit Community Cloud (*.streamlit.app), there is a strict architectural quirk with mobile devices:

How Streamlit Cloud Serves the Page:
Streamlit serves its own root HTML shell before Python code even executes. Its root <head> permanently starts with:

HTML
<title>Streamlit</title>
<link rel="icon" href="https://static.streamlit.io/favicon.svg">
Why Python st.markdown('<head>...</head>') or <script> Fails:
Streamlit sandboxes all custom HTML and components inside an <iframe> inside the page body.

iOS Safari and Android Chrome only inspect the outer parent <head> when you tap "Add to Home Screen".

Because iOS cannot see a top-level <link rel="apple-touch-icon"> in the parent head, it defaults to a screenshot or the host's favicon (the red Streamlit logo), and defaults the title to the domain name "Streamlit".

The Two Ways to Fix This
Depending on how native you want the shortcut to look, here are the two working methods:

Option 1: The Native iOS Shortcuts Method (Guaranteed 100% Custom Icon & Name)
This is the standard approach for custom-branded web apps hosted on platform services like Streamlit or HuggingFace. It bypasses Safari's platform defaults and forces iOS to use your custom icon:

