Still stuck with the same issue. That space shows the HTML code but not the finished product. See attached

The issue where raw HTML code appears inside a grey code box instead of rendering visually is caused by a rule in Markdown parsing (CommonMark):

The Root Cause: In standard Markdown, any block of text indented with 4 or more spaces (or a tab) is automatically interpreted as a pre-formatted code block (<pre><code>).

Because the HTML markup inside st.markdown(f"""...""") was indented along with the Python code (8 to 12 spaces in from the margin), Streamlit's Markdown engine bypassed rendering and displayed the raw HTML text inside a code block, even with unsafe_allow_html=True present.

Step-by-Step Fix
To resolve this completely, import Python's built-in textwrap module and wrap the multi-line HTML string in textwrap.dedent(...). This strips away the leading whitespace before the string reaches the Markdown parser, forcing the browser to render the styled cards properly.

1. Add textwrap to Imports at the Top of app.py
Near lines 1–15, ensure import textwrap is included:

