In reviewing app (7).py, the code block syntax itself is valid, but the reason HTML is printing to the screen instead of rendering comes down to two specific issues:

1. Markdown Parsing Rule (The Primary Cause)
Even when st.markdown(..., unsafe_allow_html=True) is used, Streamlit runs content through a Markdown parser first. In Markdown specification:

Any lines inside a multiline string that begin with 4 or more spaces (or tabs) are treated as an indented code block (<pre><code>).

In app (7).py, lines 938 through 954 inside the metric-card string are indented with 4 to 8 spaces (for example,     <div style="margin-bottom: 6px;"> and         <span class="badge-scenario">). The parser detects that indentation and converts the HTML into raw visible text.

2. Redundant Duplicate <head> Injection
At the top of app (7).py (lines 34–52), there is an unescaped <head> block injected via st.markdown:

Python
st.markdown(
    f"""
<head>
    <title>Fragrance Ops</title>
...
""",
    unsafe_allow_html=True,
)
Because st.set_page_config already sets the app title and the page_icon image at line 17, injecting a raw <head> tag inside the page body can break the DOM container styling that follows it.

The Clean Fix
Remove lines 34–52 (the duplicate <head> markdown block at the top of the file).

Replace the metric-card block (lines 936–957 in app (7).py) so that the HTML lines are completely flush to the left margin without any leading spaces:

