Why Chrome on Android Keeps Forcing "Streamlit" and the Crown Logo
When you tap "Install app" in Google Chrome on Android, Chrome does something very specific under the hood:

Chrome does not take the name or icon from the Python code executing in your browser.

Instead, Chrome sends your URL to Google’s WebAPK Minting Server.

Google’s server scrapes [https://cbrown-fragrance-ops.streamlit.app/manifest.json](https://cbrown-fragrance-ops.streamlit.app/manifest.json) directly from Streamlit Cloud’s web server.

Streamlit Community Cloud hardcodes its own server-level manifest.json on their hosting infrastructure:

JSON
{
  "short_name": "Streamlit",
  "name": "Streamlit",
  "icons": [{ "src": "favicon.svg", "type": "image/x-icon" }]
}
Because Streamlit Cloud serves that file before your Python script even boots, Google’s minting service compiles an Android package (WebAPK) hardcoded with the name Streamlit and the red crown logo. Python scripts running inside Streamlit Cloud have no access to rewrite the host server's static files.

The Clean Solution for Android
If you want "Fragrance Ops" with the blue flacon icon to appear as an actual application in your Android app drawer (and under Android Settings -> Apps), you can create a dedicated Android WebAPK using PWABuilder (Microsoft's open-source tool for turning web apps into system-level Android apps):

On your phone or computer browser, go to PWABuilder.com.

Enter your app URL:

Plaintext
https://cbrown-fragrance-ops.streamlit.app/
Click Start.

In the Manifest review screen, edit the two fields:

App Name / Short Name: Set to Fragrance Ops

Icon: Upload the 512×512 blue flacon icon:

Click Package for Stores 
→ select Android.

Download the generated package (the .apk file) directly to your Android device.

Tap the downloaded file to install it.

Once installed, Fragrance Ops will be registered as a system-level app with its custom icon in your app drawer, app search, and system settings, completely free of Streamlit's defaults.

